= Grade by grade

Each grade band from VB to V8+ gets a guide in the same six parts, from what problems look like to why people stall. Read the guide for your grade and the one above it. If you climb most V4s, read the V4 guide, the step to V5 (@grades-v4-v5) and the V5 guide.

*Indoor grades are local.* #ix("Grades!indoor variation") A #jt("setter") (the person who designs a problem) grades it against the other problems in the same gym, so the same grade can feel different elsewhere #src("F27"). Many walls grade by #jt("circuit") (a colour that spans two or three grades) #src("F26"). #ix("Circuits") Treat the guides as a picture of what is typical, not as a test. Chapter 2 explains how gym grades vary. The appendix converts V-grades to #jt("Font grade", body: [Font grades]) (the European scale).

*The badges.* Each band lists every technique whose Part II badge, such as #badge(4), falls at that grade. The badges are judgement. No coaching scheme, such as the UK's NICAS, ties named techniques to V-grades #src("F28", "F32"). The nearest thing is the technique clinics gyms run for named grade bands, and this chapter leans on those #src("F33", "F34", "F35").

*The physical side.* The strength numbers are averages from a large database of tested climbers #src("F1"). Climbers at the same grade differ widely. The text flags any number that is a reconstruction or an extrapolation.

#notebox[How long progress takes][
#ix("Progression!time to grade")
Lattice Training#ix("Lattice Training") holds data on about 65,000 climbers. Climbers at V1–V4 report 5.8 years of climbing on average. The average rises to 8.6 years at V5–V7 and 9.7 years at V8–V11 #src("F2"). The figures are self-reported. Lattice suspects that casual long-time climbers inflate the V1–V4 figure.

Age stretches every step.#ix("Age!years to grade") At V8, climbers under 25 report 3.8 years of climbing on average. Climbers over 40 report 13.0 years #src("F2").

Lattice's coaches see plateaus#ix("Plateau!when it happens") "somewhere between V3–V6", usually in the first couple of years #src("F9"). In one forum thread, climbers' best grades after a year range from about V4 outdoors to V8 indoors #src("F47"). Forum posters are a keen, self-selected group.

Haven't climbed a V5 after a year? Then you are in the middle of the pack, not behind it.
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ VB–V0

== VB–V0 (Font~3–\u{2060}4+) <grade-0>
#fit-pages[

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at VB–V0][
  + Watch each foot onto its hold before you weight it (#tref("precise-footwork", body: [precise footwork], show-page: false)).
  + Hang on #tref("straight-arms", body: [straight arms], show-page: false) between moves; bend them only to pull.
  + Stand up on your legs; let your arms only steer.
  + Say which hand goes to each hold before you start.
  + Jump off from low down and land with bent knees.
]

==== What problems look like

The wall is vertical, a gentle #tref("slab", body: [slab]) (leaning back) or a slight #tref("overhang", body: [overhang]). The holds are #tref("jug", body: [jugs]) and deep edges you can wrap your fingers over. Each foothold takes your whole forefoot. Problems have four to eight moves, laid out like a ladder. Each reach has a foothold under it. You can stop and hang anywhere. No move needs momentum.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Three habits carry you through this band and every band after it. Place each foot exactly (#tref("precise-footwork")). Hang on #tref("straight-arms") between moves; bend them only to pull. Move up by standing on your feet, not by pulling with your arms. Learn to jump off and land too#ix("Falling!practice") (@cons-falling): NICAS and the DAV#ix("DAV") both start their schemes with how to boulder safely #src("F28", "F32").

#grade-techniques(0)

==== Focus

- Look at each foothold until your shoe is on it. Then put your weight on it. Place the inside edge of your big toe on the best part of the hold.
- Climb many different problems each session. Lattice advises 6–12 months of climbing before any finger training#ix("Fingerboard!when to start") #src("F6").
- Learn how your gym marks problems: which holds are the start, how to finish and which colour is which grade #src("F26").

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Find the start holds (often one marked hold per hand) and the finish hold.
- Count the hand moves. Say which hand goes to each hold.
- Point out the foothold for each reach. If a foothold sits right under your next handhold, step up onto it before you reach.

==== Physical side

Nothing specific. Any healthy adult has the strength for this band.

==== Why people stall here

- *Bent arms.* You climb with bent elbows and get #jt("pump", body: [pumped]) (tired, swollen forearms) after a few moves. Fix: pause on straight arms before every reach.
- *Noisy feet.* Your shoes scuff and slip off. Fix: climb whole problems placing every foot without a sound.
- *Fear of falling.* Fix: practise falling from low down, a little higher each session (@cons-falling).

Most people get through this band in their first few sessions.
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V1

== V1 (Font 5) <grade-1>
#fit-pages[

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V1][
  + Shift your hips over the pushing foot before every reach.
  + Stand on the inside #tref("edging", body: [edge], show-page: false) facing in, the outside edge when turned.
  + Press the rubber flat on the wall where no foothold exists (#tref("smearing", body: [smearing], show-page: false)).
  + Pull each hold away from the way it faces.
  + Grip each hold only as hard as it needs.
]

==== What problems look like

Walls are mostly vertical or slightly overhanging. Jugs give way to #jt("incut") edges, whose lip curls back towards you. The first holds you pull sideways, squeeze or press on appear. Footholds are smaller and sometimes off to the side, so some moves start with a weight shift before the reach. Moves are still #jt("static"): you could stop at any point. Problems have five to eight moves.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Use #tref("edging") on the outside of the shoe as well as the inside. Use #tref("smearing") (rubber pressed flat on the wall) where there is no foothold. Pull a #tref("sidepull") towards you. Many V1s ask you to bring both hands onto one hold (#tref("matching")). Others ask you to turn both knees out so your hips come in (#tref("frog")).

#grade-techniques(1)

==== Focus

- Before each reach, shift your hips sideways over the foot you will push from. Then reach.
- Use the inside edge of the shoe when you face the wall. Switch to the outside edge when your hip turns in.
- Grip no harder than you need. On easy problems, relax your fingers until they start to slip. Then add a little.

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Read which way each handhold faces. A hold that faces left puts your body to its right (Chapter 1).
- Look for footholds that bring your hips under the next handhold.
- On a pinch, find the thumb side before you set off.

==== Physical side

Nothing specific.

==== Why people stall here

- *Square-on climbing.* You keep your chest to the wall and stand on both toe tips for every move. Sideways reaches then fall short. Fix: on easy problems, practise turning one hip into the wall (#tref("hip-turn")).
- *Over-gripping.* Fix: the loose-grip habit above.
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V2

== V2 (Font 5+) <grade-2>
#fit-pages[

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V2][
  + Turn one hip into the wall on steep ground (#tref("hip-turn", body: [hip turn], show-page: false)).
  + Put out a #tref("flag", body: [flag], show-page: false) before a hand lets go, not after.
  + Lean off two sideways holds that face the same way (#tref("layback", body: [layback], show-page: false)).
  + Bring your feet high under an #tref("undercling", body: [undercling], show-page: false) before you reach.
  + Let straight arms hold you on the steep, not your biceps.
]

==== What problems look like

Gentle overhangs of about 10–25° appear. Holds include ones you pull sideways, pull up from below or push outwards. Some moves only work with your body turned sideways to the wall. Footholds no longer sit under every handhold, so you often reach from one foot while the other has nothing useful. Problems have five to ten moves. A short #jt("dynamic") move (one that uses momentum, so you can't stop halfway) may appear, but most V2s still go statically.

==== Techniques that start to matter

On the steep, learn to turn a hip in (#tref("hip-turn")); the #tref("backstep") builds on this from V3. The #tref("flag") stops the #tref("barn-door") swing when one hand lets go. Two sideways holds facing the same way ask for a #tref("layback"). An #tref("undercling") at chest height asks you to bring your feet high. Some moves ask you to swap feet on one hold (#tref("foot-swap")). A forum poster says some V2s can still be pulled on muscle #src("F44"). Avoid building that habit.

#grade-techniques(2)

==== Focus

- Learn to tell front-on moves from side-on moves. Switch between them on purpose.
- On an overhang, put the outside edge of your shoe on the foothold and turn that hip into the wall. The shoulder on that side rises, which lengthens your reach.
- When one hand is about to let go, put the opposite foot flat against the wall as a flag. Do it *before* you move.

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Two holds facing the same way, such as two sideways holds facing left, mean a layback or a backstep.
- A single foothold far out to one side, with nothing on the other side, means a flag or a backstep.

==== Physical side

Nothing specific.

==== Why people stall here

- *Barn-dooring.* You swing off when one hand releases. Fix: the flag above, *before* the hand moves.
- *Arms tire on the steep.* Fix: straighten your arms and turn a hip in. Your skeleton then holds you, not your biceps.

#ix("Plateau!at V2") V2 is a known plateau: one gym runs a movement clinic for climbers stuck there #src("F36"). The way out is learning to climb side-on.
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V3

== V3 (Font 6A/6A+) <grade-3>
#fit-pages[

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V3][
  + Catch poor holds at the top of your rise (#tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoint], show-page: false)).
  + Hook a heel on hip-high holds to pull your hips in (#tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hook], show-page: false)).
  + Drive your toes into the wall to keep your feet on overhangs.
  + Sink your hips, then drive with your legs to create momentum.
  + Spend one session in four on the style you avoid, slab or steep.
]

==== What problems look like

V3s come in two families. *Steep* problems sit on 20–40° overhangs with mid-sized edges and rounded holds. Your feet stay on only if you pull with them. *Slab and vertical* problems have blank wall, #tref("volume", body: [volumes]) (big bolted-on shapes) and small edges for the feet. There, balance is the #jt("crux") (the hardest move). Many V3s are built around one clear move, such as a long span or a hook with the heel. Problems have four to ten moves.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Learn the #tref("deadpoint"): catch the hold at the top of a small upward movement, when you weigh nothing. Use your first #tref("heel-hook") on a good hold to pull your hips in. Learn #tref("overhang") basics. Your hands meet the #tref("sloper"), the #tref("pinch") and the #tref("half-crimp").

#grade-techniques(3)

==== Focus

- *Toes into the wall on steep ground.* Push through the front of the shoe into the hold, not down along the wall #src("F41").
- *Create momentum.* Sink your hips a little, then drive up with your legs. Your arms guide the movement rather than haul it #src("F33", "F39").
- *Flash more.* Read each V3 fully before you touch it. Then try to #jt("flash") it: climb it first go, without falling.

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Can you catch the crux hold while moving, or must you arrive static (#tref("lock-off"))? You can catch a deep hold with a lip. A poor sloper needs a deadpoint or a slow reach.
- Is there a foothold at hip height on the side you move towards? It may be meant for a heel hook.
- On slab, find where you can stand. Then find blank wall clean enough to #tref("smearing", body: [smear]) on.

==== Physical side

Lattice tests a #jt("max hang")#ix("Max hang!averages by grade"): the most extra weight you can hang from a small edge for a few seconds. In its lowest band, V1–V4, climbers add about 19 kg on two arms #src("F1"). Many V3 climbers can't add that and still climb well.

==== Why people stall here

- *Feet cutting on the steep.* Fix: keep both feet pressing into the wall, even the one you are about to move. Keep your hips close #src("F41").
- *Climbing one style.*#ix("Plateau!climbing one style") You climb all the steep V3s and avoid the slabs, or the reverse. Lattice lists avoiding weak styles as a main cause of plateaus #src("F9"). Fix: spend one session in four on the style you avoid.
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V4

== V4 (Font 6B/6B+) <grade-4>
#fit-pages[

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V4][
  + Start big steep moves from straight arms, hips back and down.
  + Pull with your #tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hook], show-page: false): bend the knee and drag your hips in.
  + Use a #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hook], show-page: false) to stop the swing when a hand lets go.
  + Keep both toes pressing in when your hands reach far (#tref("body-tension", body: [body tension], show-page: false)).
  + Plan your body position for the hardest move before you start.
]

==== What problems look like

Expect overhangs of 30–45°, or vertical walls with poor feet. #jt("crimp", body: [Crimps]) (small edges held on the fingertips) and rounded holds replace most jugs. Footholds are small edges or the faces of #tref("volume", body: [volumes]). A V4 often combines two or three techniques, e.g. a heel hook that sets up a long reach. One or two moves need momentum, e.g. a jump to a big hold or a #jt("coordination move") (a timed sequence of jumps or swings). Problems have four to eight hard moves.

==== Techniques that start to matter

The #tref("heel-hook") becomes an active pull: bend the knee and pull with the #jt("hamstring") (the muscle at the back of the thigh) to draw your hips in. Add the #tref("toe-hook") to stop a swing. Use the #tref("drop-knee") on steep walls and the #tref("gaston"), a hold you push outwards with your elbow out. Keep your feet on with #tref("toe-pull") and #tref("body-tension"). Try your first #tref("dyno") and #tref("cutting-loose") moves. From here on, momentum is a normal tool, not a last resort.

#grade-techniques(4)

==== Focus

- *Choose #jt("static") or #jt("dynamic").* For big moves on overhangs, start with straight arms. Sit your hips back and down, then drive from your feet. Newer climbers tend to #tref("lock-off", body: [lock off]) too much #src("F39").
- *Body tension.* When your hands are far above you or out to the side, keep pushing through both toes. If your feet go light, pull on the footholds with your toes.
- *Repeat, then reach up.* Repeat V4s until they feel smooth. Put real attempts into at least one V5 every session. Lattice counts learning chances, not sends #src("F9").

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Find the #jt("crux") (hardest move) first, then work backwards. What body position must you be in to start the crux move?
- Look for heel-hook holds: a horizontal edge or volume at hip height, out to the side.
- Look for toe-hook features: a hold or volume edge that faces down or sideways at foot level.
- Find the move where your feet will swing off. Decide how you will catch the swing before you leave the ground.

==== Physical side

#ix("Finger strength!by grade") Lattice's V1–V4 band adds about 19 kg on a two-arm #jt("max hang") (the most extra weight you can hang briefly) #src("F1"). A forum user's reconstruction of Lattice's data for men puts V4 at about 49% of bodyweight on one arm, on a 20 mm edge. That is an estimate, not a published figure #src("F15").

==== Why people stall here

- *Muscling through.* You can pull hard, so you never learn the position that makes the move easy.
- *Never trying V5s.* You repeat what you can already climb.

The next section covers both.
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V4 to V5

== From V4 to V5 <grades-v4-v5>
#fit-pages[

#remember[Five things that get you from V4 to V5][
  + Assume technique, not strength, stops you until a symptom says otherwise.
  + Work three to five V5s, in different styles, over several sessions.
  + After each fall, check your hips, your off foot and your arms.
  + Start steep moves on straight arms and drive from your feet; don't #tref("lock-off", body: [lock off], show-page: false).
  + Drill #tref("drop-knee", body: [drop knees], show-page: false), toe hooks and #tref("compression", body: [compression], show-page: false) on V3s and V4s first.
]

#ix("Plateau!V4 to V5", main: true)
This section is for you if you climb most V4s but haven't #jt("send", body: [sent]) a V5 (climbed one cleanly). The V5 plateau is common enough that gyms name clinics after it #src("F33", "F35").

==== What changes between V4 and V5

In the table, the #jt("crux") is the hardest move and #jt("beta") is the sequence of moves that works.

#gtable(
  columns: (auto, 1fr, 1fr),
  table.header(th[], th[Typical V4], th[Typical V5]),
  [*Holds*], [Edges and rounded holds with some lip; a jug to rest on], [Smaller #jt("crimp", body: [crimps]) (fingertip edges), rounder holds, pinches; few rests #src("F48", "F46")],
  [*Wall angle*], [30–45°, or vertical with poor feet], [More problems on the steepest walls, 40–50° (judgement)],
  [*Footholds*], [Small edges, volumes], [Smaller edges or none; tension holds the feet on #src("F48")\; feet often swing off],
  [*The crux*], [Several solutions work], [Usually one technique done precisely, such as a #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hook]) to stop a swing (judgement)],
  [*Dynamic moves*], [Deadpoints, small jumps], [Committed #tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoints]), #tref("dyno", body: [dynos]) and coordination moves such as #tref("run-and-jump") #src("F34")],
  [*Body tension*], [Helps], [Needed to keep the feet on through hand moves #src("F35", "F48")],
  [*Techniques per problem*], [Two or three], [Three to five, linked without rests (judgement)],
  [*How it goes*], [First go, or in one session], [Often several sessions; you work out the beta move by move (judgement)],
)

Most rows come from gym clinics and forum reports, not measurement. The forum reports are about the step to V6, which shows where V5 is heading.

==== What holds people at V4

+ *Strength instead of position.* Lattice's#ix("Lattice Training") coaches say strong climbers learn technique more slowly, because they can muscle through moves #src("F9"). After each fall, ask: "If I'm strong enough for this, what am I missing?" #src("F42")
+ *Locking off on the steep.* Newer climbers hold bent-arm #tref("lock-off", body: [lock-offs]) too often. Start big moves from straight arms instead, hips back and down. Press into the foot rather than pulling the knee in tight #src("F39").
+ *Staying tight to the wall when you need to turn.* Pinned to the wall, you can't rotate, so you swing when a hand comes off. Before you let go with the lower hand, relax away from it. Find the point where you balance, then move #src("F40").
+ *Ignoring the off foot.* The #jt("off foot") is the foot not carrying you. It controls rotation. Push it into the wall at roughly right angles to the line from your hand to your foot. Touch it briefly on the wall to stop a swing #src("F41"). #ix("Off foot")
+ *Avoiding weak styles and limit attempts.*#ix("Plateau!climbing one style") Lattice lists both among the main causes of plateaus #src("F9").
+ *Missing a few techniques.* Gym clinics for the "V5 plateau" teach a short list: #tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hooks]), toe hooks, #tref("flag", body: [flagging]), #tref("body-tension", body: [body tension]), #tref("compression", body: [compression]), deadpoints and sequencing #src("F35"). A V5–V6 clinic adds #tref("drop-knee", body: [drop knees]), #tref("mantle", body: [mantles]), #tref("rockover", body: [rockovers]) and dynos #src("F34").

==== Technique or strength?

The finger-strength gap between V4 and V5 is small. On a two-arm #jt("max hang") (the most extra weight you can hang for a few seconds), Lattice's averages rise from about 19 kg in its V1–V4 band to about 24 kg at V5–V7 #src("F1"). The forum reconstruction for men goes from about 49% to 55% of bodyweight on one arm, again an estimate #src("F15").

Lattice's coaches advise climbers with under five years of experience to learn to apply the strength they have, rather than only getting stronger #src("F14"). One study of 55 advanced and elite women#ix("Studies!finger strength and grade")#ix("Women climbers") found that finger strength explained about half the variation in their bouldering grades #src("F16"). Upper-body power explained a similar share. The study didn't measure technique.

The extra strength for V5 mostly comes from trying V5s. For when finger training makes sense, see @cons-adult.

==== Find your limiter

#ix("Limiter, finding your")
Try three or four V5s in different styles. Note *how* you fail on each.

#gtable(
  columns: (1.25fr, 1fr, 1fr),
  table.header(th[Symptom on a V5], th[Most likely limiter], th[Where to look]),
  [You can't hold the start or crux holds, even with good feet], [Finger strength for that kind of hold], [#tref("half-crimp"), #tref("sloper"), #tref("pinch")],
  [You can do every move alone but can't link them], [Efficiency and pacing. Some climbers can't string several near-limit moves together #src("F14")], [#tref("straight-arms")\; re-read the problem and look for rests],
  [Your feet swing off and won't go back on], [Body tension, toe hook, flag, off foot #src("F35", "F41")], [#tref("body-tension"), #tref("toe-hook"), #tref("cutting-loose")],
  [You fall the moment a hand lets go], [Rotation and body position #src("F40")], [#tref("drop-knee"), #tref("backstep"), #tref("barn-door")],
  [You stall in a lock-off just short of the hold], [Static or dynamic? #src("F39")], [#tref("deadpoint"), #tref("hip-pump")],
  [A climber your height does it easily with other beta], [Beta and technique], [Copy their sequence. Then learn the technique behind it],
  [You rarely try V5s, or stop after two goes], [Tactics and mindset #src("F9")], [The plan below],
)

The table follows Lattice's logic: compare what your fingers can hold with what you can climb #src("F14"). The categories are judgement.

==== A plan for the next months

+ Choose three to five V5s: one steep, one slab or vertical, one dynamic, one compression. Work them over several sessions.
+ Break each one into single moves. Once every move goes, link pairs, then threes, then the whole problem.
+ After each fall, check three things. Where were your hips? Where was your off foot? Were your arms straight before the move? #src("F39", "F41", "F42")
+ Practise the clinic techniques listed above on V3s and V4s. There you have attention to spare for the movement #src("F34", "F35").
+ Spend most of each session on the steepest wall, on problems of four to eight moves at your limit #src("F43", "F46").
+ Copy beta from climbers close to your height. A short climber's beta often won't fit a tall body.

==== Reading V5 problems

- Find the crux by its holds: the smallest hold, the longest gap or the hold facing the "wrong" way.
- Ask what the crux hold wants. If you pull it from below, you can do it statically. A #tref("sidepull") or #tref("gaston") needs a body position. A sloper wants low hips and straight arms.
- Before you start, find the hidden footholds, such as a volume's edge or a hold's side.
- Find the swing move, where a hand lets go and the feet are poor. Decide in advance: flag, toe hook, or let the feet swing and catch them.
- Read in chunks of two to four holds (Chapter 2). Research on routes links this kind of preview to better climbing on hard routes #src("F11").

]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V5

== V5 (Font 6C/6C+) <grade-5>
#fit-pages[

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V5][
  + Place each foot before you move a hand.
  + Find the one technique the hardest move demands, then do it exactly.
  + Climb V5s in every style before you chase V6.
  + Give each new V5 one real first-go attempt before you work it.
  + Expect moves that leave the wall: learn the #tref("double-dyno", body: [double dyno], show-page: false) and #tref("campus-move", body: [campus move], show-page: false).
]

==== What problems look like

Expect steep walls with small #jt("crimp", body: [crimps]) (fingertip edges), poor rounded holds and pinches. Footholds are poor or missing. Rests are few. The table in @grades-v4-v5 compares V5 with V4 in detail.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Some moves leave the wall completely: the #tref("double-dyno"), the #tref("campus-move") and #tref("foot-catch"). Steep hooking extends to the #tref("bicycle"), the #tref("heel-toe-cam") and, where a volume or corner allows it, the #tref("knee-bar"). Squeezing between two holds becomes a technique of its own (#tref("compression")). #jt("comp style", body: [Comp-style]) problems (set like competition problems) add the #tref("tic-tac"), the #tref("pogo") and the #tref("double-clutch").

#grade-techniques(5)

==== Focus

- *Build a V5 base.* Once your first V5s go, climb many V5s in different styles. The grade should stop depending on style.
- *Flash attempts.* Read a new V5 fully. Give it one real #jt("flash") attempt (a try to climb it first go) before you work it.
- *Finish the technique list.* Practise the V5–V6 clinic techniques on V4s first #src("F34").

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Decide where each foot goes *before* each hand move. At V5 the feet decide whether the hand move works.
- The fuller list for your first V5s is in @grades-v4-v5.

==== Physical side

Lattice's V5–V7 band adds about 24 kg on a two-arm #jt("max hang") (the most extra weight you can hang briefly) #src("F1"). The forum reconstruction puts men at V5 around 55% of bodyweight on one arm, on a 20 mm edge (an estimate) #src("F15").

==== Why people stall here

- *Staying on the V5s that suit you.* It feels like progress, but your weak styles stay weak.
- *V6s feel like a different sport.* One forum climber found V6s "too overhung and crimpy or too shouldery". Replies pointed to overhang technique and finger strength for small holds. Others said each gym's setting has blind spots, so visit other gyms #src("F46").
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V6

== V6 (Font 7A) <grade-6>
#fit-pages[

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V6][
  + Climb the style you avoid, usually steep walls with small edges.
  + Use #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hooks], show-page: false) and heel hooks as main holds, not extras.
  + Stop the swing and replace your feet without letting go (#tref("cutting-loose", body: [cutting loose], show-page: false)).
  + Squeeze with both arms and both feet on #tref("compression", body: [compression], show-page: false) problems.
  + Visit other gyms to find the problem types you lack.
]

==== What problems look like

Expect walls of 40–50°, roofs, or very technical vertical faces. Holds are small #jt("crimp", body: [crimps]) (fingertip edges), poor rounded holds and wide pinches. Some problems make you squeeze between two holds or volumes. Expect one or two big dynamic moves, or long runs of medium-hard moves.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Hooks become main holds, not just aids. #tref("compression") uses both arms and both feet. You learn #tref("cutting-loose"): stopping the swing and putting your feet back on without losing your hands. The #tref("paddle-dyno"), a signature #jt("comp style", body: [comp-style]) move (from competition setting), appears.

#grade-techniques(6)

==== Focus

- Work on your weak styles, usually overhangs and small holds. Climb in other gyms to find them #src("F46").
- Pick #jt("project", body: [projects]) (problems you work over several sessions) and stay with them.
- Be precise about where each finger and toe goes #src("F38").

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Find the places where the feet are low and far from the hands. Those need the most tension.
- Find the hooking features and the target hold of each dynamic move.
- Read the texture of each rounded hold and which way it grips best.

==== Physical side

Finger strength becomes a common limiter. Forum climbers recommend finger training to break into V6 #src("F46"). The reconstruction puts men at V6 around 61% of bodyweight on one arm, on a 20 mm edge (an estimate) #src("F15").

==== Why people stall here

- *Climbing V6 like V5.* Solutions that worked at V5 are too loose here.
- *Holds too small to use.* Your fingers can't yet hold the small crimps.
- *A comfortable home gym.* It hides your weakest style. Fix: the focus points above.
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V7

== V7 (Font 7A+) <grade-7>
#fit-pages[

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V7][
  + Test for your weaknesses instead of guessing them.
  + Catch each hold fast and exactly as you arrive (#tref("latching", body: [latching], show-page: false)).
  + Try two or three foot options for each hard move.
  + Decide which way to turn your hand on every poor hold.
  + Expect a hard problem to take weeks, not sessions.
]

==== What problems look like

The terrain is the same as V6 with less margin. Holds are a size smaller and feet are worse. Moves are longer or more powerful. Many climbers at this level measure themselves on #jt("training board", body: [training boards]) such as the MoonBoard, Kilter and Tension: standard steep walls with a fixed grid of holds. Gym setting borrows their style.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Few new techniques appear. What changes is how exactly you do the ones you know. Compression must hold for longer. Toe and heel hooks must grip poor features. You must #tref("latching", body: [latch]) holds fast and accurately at the end of a dynamic move. Lattice calls this #jt("contact strength"): catching a hold quickly as you arrive #src("F14"). #ix("Contact strength")

#grade-techniques(7)

==== Focus

- Find your weaknesses by testing them, not by guessing #src("F14").
- Choose #jt("project", body: [projects]) (problems you work over many sessions) that take weeks.

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Work out fine #jt("beta") (the exact sequence of moves). Decide which way to turn a hand on a hold and exactly where a toe goes.
- Try two or three foot options for each hard hand move before you commit to one.

==== Physical side

The reconstruction puts men at V7 around 67% of bodyweight on one arm, on a 20 mm edge (an estimate) #src("F15"). Men at V7–V8 average about 145% of bodyweight on Lattice's two-rep weighted pull-up test #src("F5").

==== Why people stall here

Some climbers test strong on a #jt("hangboard") (a board of edges to hang from) but climb below that level. Lattice says they may lack contact strength, or the power for several hard moves in a row #src("F14").
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V8+

== V8 and above (Font~7B~and~up) <grade-8>
#fit-pages[

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V8 and above][
  + Work out sequences for your own body, not an average one.
  + Build strength on the exact grips your hardest problems use.
  + Plan rests and attempts for every session on a hard problem.
  + Expect each new grade to take seasons, not weeks.
  + Keep placing your feet as precisely as you did at V0.
]

==== What problems look like

Expect hard versions of everything above: the steepest walls, and holds that only work in one exact position. Many problems copy the style of #jt("training board", body: [training boards]) (standard steep walls with fixed holds). Sequences are long and powerful (judgement).

==== Techniques that start to matter

This book's catalogue has nothing new for this band. Progress comes from strength on specific grips and from #jt("beta") (move sequences) worked out for your body.

#grade-techniques(8)

==== Focus

Build strength for specific grips, pinches and squeezing. Refine your tactics.

==== Reading problems at this grade

Your build drives the solutions. Read for *your* body, not an average one.

==== Physical side

Lattice's V8–V11 band adds about 38 kg on a two-arm #jt("max hang") (the most extra weight you can hang briefly) #src("F1"). Climbers in that band train 4.4 hours a week #src("F3"). The reconstruction puts men at V8 around 73% of bodyweight on one arm (an estimate) #src("F15"). Above roughly 160–165% of bodyweight on the weighted pull-up test, Lattice sees diminishing returns from more pulling strength in men #src("F5").

==== Why people stall here

Time and age hold people here: see the box on progress at the start of this chapter. Structured training helps: a 51-year-old Lattice client sent V8 with it #src("F13") (see @cons-adult on training as an adult).
]

